  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "plant fusion protein powder",
      "plantfusion protein",
      "plantfusion complete protein",
      "plantfusion review",
      "is plantfusion third party tested"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Plant Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-plant-based-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">PlantFusion Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      PlantFusion sells a short-label, allergen-free pea protein that buyers looking for a clean vegan scoop keep finding. Here is what is on the label, what the brand does and does not prove about testing, and what each 25 g of protein costs.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">PlantFusion Products</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Is It Third-Party Tested?</a>
      <a href="#whats-in-it">What Is in Complete Protein?</a>
      <a href="#sweeteners">Sweeteners and Stevia</a>
      <a href="#cost-per-25g">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#buyer-feedback">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#vs-competitors">PlantFusion vs Rivals</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          PlantFusion Complete Protein is a clean-label pea protein with 21 g of protein and 120 calories per scoop, but it carries no NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Clean Label Project seal that we could find. Whether that matters depends on who you are: a drug-tested athlete should buy a certified powder, while a dairy-free shopper mainly needs a short label and a fair price. This review walks through the four main PlantFusion protein powders, the sweeteners, the testing question and the cost per 25 g.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. PlantFusion Protein Powder Review: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            PlantFusion Complete Protein is a sensible, allergen-friendly vegan protein that is cheaper per gram than most premium plant brands when bought in the 75-serving bag, but it is not the pick for anyone who needs independent proof of purity. The label is short, the protein comes mostly from pea protein isolate, there is no added sugar, and the brand says it is certified non-GMO and gluten free. What it does not have is a sport certification or a published heavy-metal panel we could find.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy it if you want a dairy-free, soy-free and rice-free scoop with 21 g of protein, you like a sweeter shake, or you need the unsweetened Natural - No Stevia version for baking and smoothies. Skip it if you are drug-tested in sport, if you want proof of low lead and cadmium before you buy, or if 21 g per serving feels light next to plant powders that reach 30 g.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Complete Protein, per scoop (label)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">21 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">120</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Added sugar</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$2.06</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g, 75-serving bag</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Which PlantFusion Protein Powder Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            PlantFusion sells more than a dozen powders, but four are protein powders in the usual sense. They are ranked below by how useful they are to a typical buyer, with the cost per 25 g of protein worked out from the brand-site prices on 21 November 2026. Nutrition figures are from the brand page or a retailer copy of the label at the time of writing and can vary by flavor.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is PlantFusion Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Partly, by the brand's own description, but not in any way a shopper can verify from a public database. We searched the NSF Certified for Sport product list, the Informed Sport search and the Clean Label Project site on 21 November 2026 and found no PlantFusion product in any of them. None of the protein pages on the brand site carries an NSF, Informed Choice or Clean Label Project seal either.
          </p>

          <p>
            What the brand does say is narrower. The PlantFusion Organic Protein page states that the product is tested by third parties for purity and potency and tested negative for the eight major food allergens. The Complete Lean and Inspire for Women pages say the products are made in an NSF/GMP-certified facility. Those are real claims, but they are not the same thing. A GMP-certified facility means the factory follows good manufacturing practice; it does not mean each finished tub was screened for banned substances or contaminants by an independent body that publishes the result.
          </p>

          <p>
            That distinction matters most for two groups. Athletes in tested sport need a lot-tested certification, because a single contaminated batch can end a season, and PlantFusion does not offer one. Shoppers worried about heavy metals in plant protein need test results, because pea, rice and other plant proteins pull lead and cadmium from soil, and cocoa-flavored versions tend to carry more. PlantFusion does not publish a heavy-metal panel on its product pages that we could find, so the honest answer is that the brand does not state its levels.
          </p>

          <p>
            If testing is your deciding factor, the practical move is to email PlantFusion for a recent certificate of analysis for your flavor and lot, or to choose a certified plant protein instead. Our guide to the <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">best protein powder without heavy metals</a> lists brands that publish their results, and the <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">best NSF-certified protein powders</a> covers the sport-certified options.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE TESTING ANSWER</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">GMP facility and brand testing, yes. Public sport or contaminant certification, no.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">At the time of writing, no PlantFusion product appears in the NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Clean Label Project listings we searched. The Organic line says it is third-party tested for purity and potency, but no results are published on the product page.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="whats-in-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in PlantFusion Complete Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            PlantFusion Complete Protein lists 21 g of protein and 120 calories per scoop, per the brand page at the time of writing. A retailer copy of the label lists the scoop at 30 g, with about 2 g of carbohydrate and 3 g of fat, and the brand says there is no added sugar. That works out to roughly 70 percent protein by weight, which is in line with other pea-based blends and below the 80 to 90 percent that a whey isolate reaches.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein blend is led by pea protein isolate, followed by artichoke powder, sprouted organic amaranth powder, sprouted organic quinoa powder and whole algae protein, per the brand. PlantFusion then adds four free amino acids: L-isoleucine, L-leucine, L-valine and L-glutamine. The first three are the branched-chain amino acids, added to lift the leucine content that pea protein is sometimes criticised for. The amaranth, quinoa and algae are there in small amounts and round out the amino acid profile more than they add protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 100 mg enzyme blend of bromelain and alpha-galactosidase sits alongside the protein. Bromelain is a protein-digesting enzyme from pineapple; alpha-galactosidase is the enzyme sold in anti-gas products and helps break down the bean sugars that can cause bloating with legume proteins. The brand does not claim a measured digestion benefit for this dose, and there is no strong evidence either way at 100 mg, but it is a reasonable inclusion for a pea product.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sodium is the figure to watch. The brand FAQ says the sodium is naturally occurring from the protein blend rather than added salt and comes to about 16 percent of the daily value, and a retailer copy of the label lists 390 mg per scoop. That is normal for pea protein isolate, which is processed with salts, but two shakes a day put you near 800 mg before food. If you are on a low-sodium diet, compare it with the options in our <a href="/best-low-sodium-protein-powder/">low-sodium protein powder guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            On allergens the label is unusually clean. PlantFusion says Complete Protein contains no dairy, soy, rice, eggs, nuts, tree nuts, fish or shellfish, and that it is certified non-GMO and gluten free. Rice-free is worth noting: many vegan blends use brown rice protein, which is the plant protein most often linked to arsenic, so a pea-led blend without rice sidesteps that particular concern.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sweeteners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Does PlantFusion Contain Stevia or Other Sweeteners?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, the flavored versions of PlantFusion Complete Protein contain stevia, and the brand also sells an unsweetened Natural - No Stevia flavor. PlantFusion calls its sweetening system Flavor Pure and describes it as a blend of monk fruit, lucuma fruit, yacon root and a pinch of stevia. Retailer copies of the Complete Protein label list stevia extract (Reb A), luo han guo extract, which is monk fruit, lucuma powder and yacon powder, alongside natural flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is no sucralose, acesulfame potassium or aspartame on the labels we checked, and the brand says there is no added sugar. Lucuma and yacon are fruit and root powders used for a rounder, caramel-like sweetness; yacon also contributes some fructooligosaccharides, a prebiotic fiber. In practice the sweetness comes mostly from stevia and monk fruit, which is why some buyers describe the shake as very sweet.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Organic Protein line is more restrained. Its Creamy Vanilla Bean and Rich Chocolate flavors contain a slight amount of stevia as part of the flavor system, per the brand, and its Unflavored version is stevia free. The Complete Lean page describes the same Flavor Pure blend without naming every sweetener, and its FAQ discusses erythritol, so check the tub if you avoid sugar alcohols.
          </p>

          <p>
            If stevia is the reason you are reading this, the Natural - No Stevia flavor of Complete Protein is the one to buy, and it sells at the same price as the sweetened flavors. For other unsweetened options, see our guide to the <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-stevia/">best protein powder without stevia</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-per-25g" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Much Does PlantFusion Cost per 25 g of Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            PlantFusion Complete Protein costs $2.06 per 25 g of protein in the 75-serving bag and $2.78 per 25 g in the 30-serving bag, at the brand-site prices we saw on 21 November 2026. The 15-serving bag is the worst value of the regular sizes at $3.49 per 25 g. These figures use the brand's 21 g of protein per scoop and its listed serving counts, and they exclude shipping, subscription discounts and retailer sales, which can move the price a lot.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rest of the range is more expensive per gram because each scoop carries less protein or more add-ins. Organic Protein lists 18 g per serving and costs $3.64 per 25 g in the 21-serving bag. Inspire for Women lists 18 g of pea protein and works out to about $4.63 per 25 g. Complete Lean, with 21 g of protein plus 9 g of fiber and blood-sugar ingredients, is about $4.17 per 25 g in the 20-serving size.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">PlantFusion product and size</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Price (brand site, 21 Nov 2026)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein per serving</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost per 25 g</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Complete Protein, 75 servings</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$129.97</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$2.06</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
